      "First Manned Orbit of the Moon\nDecember 21, 1968 • Cape Canaveral, Florida, U.S.A.\nIn the beginning\nGod...\nSIX CENTS • UNITED STATES\nCAP\nDEC\n21\n1983\n32920\nUSA\n20c",
      "International Tributes to the History of Flight\nFirst Manned Orbit of the Moon\nMan's drive out of the universe began on the morn-\nThe full-color cachet pictures the tense moment\ning of December 21, 1968. The Apollo 8 spacecraft,\njust before the firing of the main engine that would\nwith Col. Frank Borman, Capt. James Lovell, Jr. and\nreturn the astronauts safely to earth. Apollo 8 carried\nMajor William Anders aboard, lifted off from Cape\nno back-up parts for three vital components of that\nCanaveral, Florida at 7:51 a.m. The crew hoped to do\nengine.\nwhat no men had ever done before, break free of the\nearth's gravity and circle the moon.\nMint stamp: The mint stamp on this Cover, honoring\nthe Apollo 8 mission, was issued in 1969. It depicts an\nIt was Christmas Eve when Apollo 8 completed its\n\"earthrise\" seen from the moon and recalls the astro-\n230,000-mile lunar journey. Then, for the first time\nnauts' dramatic Christmas Eve reading from Genesis.\nsince manned space flights began, ground crews lost\nradio contact with the craft as it traveled behind the\nPostmark: This Cover was postmarked in Cape Ca-\nmoon. After ten complete lunar orbits, Apollo 8\nnaveral, Florida on December 21, 1983, the anniver-\nheaded home to a splashdown in the Pacific.\nsary of the first manned orbit of the moon mission.\n© Postal Commemorative Society\nPrinted in U.S.A.\n20/5.056.044"
